 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Credit cards are about 17 percentage points above the cash rate. I couldn't get a current personal loan rate.**\n\n- **Cash rate:** 4.36% in September 2026 (monthly average target). It was 0.10% from late 2021 to April 2022, peaked at 4.35% through 2024, fell to 3.60% in late 2025, and has risen since February 2026.\n- **Credit cards (standard advertised rate):** 21.24% in September 2026, so the spread over the cash rate is about 16.9 points. The rate was 19.94% from early 2020 until January 2023, then rose in steps to 20.07% (late 2023), 20.78% (late 2024), 20.99% (early 2025), and 21.24% (September 2026).\n- **Personal loans (unsecured, fixed advertised rate):** The RBA table I can access has this series only up to February 2020, when it was 12.46%, about 11.7 points above the 0.75% cash rate then. I could not find a more recent value, so I can't say how the current spread compares.\n\nThe card rate barely moves with the cash rate. Between late 2021 and now, the cash rate rose by about 4.3 points, while the advertised card rate rose by only about 1.3 points. Card rates tend to lag and rise in small steps, and they usually fall much more slowly than the cash rate.\n\nThese are advertised rates. The rates people actually pay on new loans are lower and are in RBA table F6, which I can pull if you want them.",
